1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:05,150
Lim Boon Keng: The Reformer
Whose Name Is Now an MRT Station

2
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:09,713
Every morning, thousands of commuters
tap in at Boon Keng station on

3
00:00:09,793 --> 00:00:14,300
the North East Line, pass under
the name, and think nothing of it.

4
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:18,501
The name is a man's. Lim Boon
Keng (1869 to 1957) was a doctor

5
00:00:18,581 --> 00:00:22,230
who treated the poor free
of charge, the leading voice

6
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:26,027
against the opium trade, a
lawmaker for fourteen years,

7
00:00:26,107 --> 00:00:31,275
a co-founder of a girls' school, of
three banks and of an insurance company,

8
00:00:31,355 --> 00:00:35,003
the personal physician of
Sun Yat-sen, and for sixteen

9
00:00:35,083 --> 00:00:38,950
years the unsalaried president
of a university in China.

10
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:43,725
His contemporaries called him
the Grand Old Man of Singapore.

11
00:00:43,725 --> 00:00:46,460
Today, almost nobody hurrying through

12
00:00:46,540 --> 00:00:49,050
his station could say who he was.

13
00:00:49,050 --> 00:00:54,100
Lim was a third-generation
Peranakan, a Straits-born Chinese.

14
00:00:54,100 --> 00:00:59,359
His grandfather, Lim Mah Peng, had
emigrated from Fujian to Penang

15
00:00:59,439 --> 00:01:05,184
in 1839 and married a Straits-born
woman; the family moved to Singapore,

16
00:01:05,264 --> 00:01:09,875
where Lim Boon Keng was born
on the 18th of October 1869.

17
00:01:09,875 --> 00:01:13,074
His childhood was shadowed
by loss: his mother died

18
00:01:13,154 --> 00:01:16,352
when he was about eight,
and his father died during

19
00:01:16,432 --> 00:01:19,775
his school years, leaving
the family short of money.

20
00:01:19,775 --> 00:01:22,807
He entered Raffles Institution in 1879,

21
00:01:22,887 --> 00:01:27,754
and when the fees ran out it was
the principal, R. W. Hullett,

22
00:01:27,834 --> 00:01:31,025
who intervened so the boy could stay on.

23
00:01:31,025 --> 00:01:33,931
By the standard accounts,
it was those early

24
00:01:34,011 --> 00:01:36,725
deaths that turned him towards medicine.

25
00:01:36,725 --> 00:01:41,427
In 1887 Lim sat the examination
for the Queen's Scholarship,

26
00:01:41,507 --> 00:01:44,775
the colony's great prize for a schoolboy.

27
00:01:44,775 --> 00:01:46,650
He came second.

28
00:01:46,650 --> 00:01:50,610
First place went to another
Raffles boy, Song Ong Siang —

29
00:01:50,690 --> 00:01:54,934
who had also come first the year
before, and was disqualified

30
00:01:55,014 --> 00:01:58,700
both times for being under
sixteen, the minimum age.

31
00:01:58,700 --> 00:02:01,512
So the scholarship went to the runner-up,

32
00:02:01,592 --> 00:02:04,625
and Lim became the
first Chinese to win it.

33
00:02:04,625 --> 00:02:08,444
Song won it properly in 1888
and read law; the two men

34
00:02:08,524 --> 00:02:11,982
stayed collaborators for
the rest of their lives,

35
00:02:12,062 --> 00:02:16,250
and much of what Lim built,
he built with Song beside him.

36
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:21,802
At the University of Edinburgh, Lim
read medicine from 1888 to 1892,

37
00:02:21,882 --> 00:02:27,932
graduating with first-class honours
in medicine — by BiblioAsia's account,

38
00:02:28,012 --> 00:02:32,650
the only one of 204 graduates
that year to take a first.

39
00:02:32,650 --> 00:02:37,431
He was a Fellow and one-time president
of the Royal Medical Society,

40
00:02:37,511 --> 00:02:41,800
and went on to Cambridge as a
research scholar in pathology.

41
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:44,980
He came home in 1893, aged twenty-four,

42
00:02:45,060 --> 00:02:50,075
about as qualified as a young
doctor in the colony could be.

43
00:02:50,075 --> 00:02:53,071
Lim set up in private practice

44
00:02:53,151 --> 00:02:56,125
on Telok Ayer Street in 1893.

45
00:02:56,125 --> 00:03:00,348
In 1896 he went into partnership
with T. Murray Robertson,

46
00:03:00,428 --> 00:03:05,023
co-owning The Dispensary at
Raffles Place; later he founded the

47
00:03:05,103 --> 00:03:10,000
Sincere Dispensary on Hill Street,
with a branch on Chulia Street.

48
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:13,415
What the National Library
Board's profile of him states

49
00:03:13,495 --> 00:03:16,465
plainly is that in his
practice he "offered free

50
00:03:16,545 --> 00:03:19,850
medical treatment to those
who could not afford it".

51
00:03:19,850 --> 00:03:23,736
From 1907 to 1910 he also
taught at the Straits and

52
00:03:23,816 --> 00:03:27,624
Federated Malay States
Government Medical School —

53
00:03:27,704 --> 00:03:31,825
the ancestor of today's
medical school — without pay.

54
00:03:31,825 --> 00:03:34,996
What opium was doing to
the Chinese community

55
00:03:35,076 --> 00:03:38,400
troubled him from his
first years in practice.

56
00:03:38,400 --> 00:03:42,352
He founded the Anti-Opium Society
and opened an Opium Refuge,

57
00:03:42,432 --> 00:03:46,053
where addicts could get medical
help to break the habit,

58
00:03:46,133 --> 00:03:49,357
and he wrote against the
trade in the newspapers —

59
00:03:49,437 --> 00:03:53,124
to the anger, the heritage
profile notes, of the colonial

60
00:03:53,204 --> 00:03:56,575
authorities and the merchants
who profited from it.

61
00:03:56,575 --> 00:04:00,596
His 1898 essay "The attitude
of the state towards

62
00:04:00,676 --> 00:04:04,275
the opium habit" set
out the case in print.

63
00:04:04,275 --> 00:04:09,058
Opium was the cause he is most
identified with, but not the only one:

64
00:04:09,138 --> 00:04:14,414
he campaigned as well against the mui
tsai system of female child servitude,

65
00:04:14,494 --> 00:04:17,525
against foot-binding,
and against gambling.

66
00:04:17,525 --> 00:04:21,903
In 1895, aged twenty-six,
Lim was appointed a Chinese

67
00:04:21,983 --> 00:04:26,525
member of the Straits
Settlements Legislative Council.

68
00:04:26,525 --> 00:04:29,826
Governor Sir Charles Mitchell
thought him too young

69
00:04:29,906 --> 00:04:34,268
and too much of a reformist firebrand,
and opposed the appointment;

70
00:04:34,348 --> 00:04:37,000
strong public support carried it anyway.

71
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:41,669
He served until 1903, resigned,
and was reappointed in

72
00:04:41,749 --> 00:04:46,770
1915 when Tan Jiak Kim gave
up his seat, serving this time

73
00:04:46,850 --> 00:04:51,775
until he left Singapore in
1921 — fourteen years in all.

74
00:04:51,775 --> 00:04:56,138
The causes he pushed were consistent
with the rest of his life:

75
00:04:56,218 --> 00:05:01,498
laws to relieve poor housing and
overcrowding, to improve public sanitation,

76
00:05:01,578 --> 00:05:05,950
to mitigate poverty, and to curb
the opium trade and gambling.

77
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:09,570
In 1896 he headed a Commission of Inquiry

78
00:05:09,650 --> 00:05:13,350
into the sources of poverty in Singapore.

79
00:05:13,350 --> 00:05:16,921
Around the Council sat a
cluster of other offices,

80
00:05:17,001 --> 00:05:20,498
all before he was thirty:
municipal commissioner,

81
00:05:20,578 --> 00:05:24,375
Chinese Advisory Board
member, Justice of the Peace.

82
00:05:24,375 --> 00:05:28,286
In 1918 he was appointed
OBE for his services

83
00:05:28,366 --> 00:05:31,825
as an unofficial member of the Council.

84
00:05:31,825 --> 00:05:35,017
Lim and Song Ong Siang
were the centre of a

85
00:05:35,097 --> 00:05:39,051
circle of English-educated
Straits Chinese reformers,

86
00:05:39,131 --> 00:05:42,175
and the 1890s were their building years.

87
00:05:42,175 --> 00:05:47,898
In 1896 Lim had a hand in forming
the Chinese Philomathic Society,

88
00:05:47,978 --> 00:05:52,375
an intellectual group
centred on reformist ideals.

89
00:05:52,375 --> 00:05:56,442
In 1897 he and Song founded
The Straits Chinese Magazine,

90
00:05:56,522 --> 00:06:02,191
an English-language quarterly that became
the platform for his essays on opium,

91
00:06:02,271 --> 00:06:06,702
on female education and on a
revived, modernised Confucianism;

92
00:06:06,782 --> 00:06:11,875
he wrote in it under pen names
including "Wen Ching" and "Historicus".

93
00:06:11,875 --> 00:06:15,943
In 1899 the two men, with other
prominent Straits Chinese,

94
00:06:16,023 --> 00:06:19,949
founded the Singapore Chinese
Girls' School — at a time,

95
00:06:20,029 --> 00:06:25,599
the heritage profile says, when education
for girls "was considered unnecessary

96
00:06:25,679 --> 00:06:30,400
and of no consequence" — and Lim
served as the school's president.

97
00:06:30,400 --> 00:06:33,537
The same year, he began
Mandarin classes at his

98
00:06:33,617 --> 00:06:36,616
own home for English-educated
Chinese who had

99
00:06:36,696 --> 00:06:40,050
never learned the language
of their grandparents.

100
00:06:40,050 --> 00:06:43,650
His most visible campaign was over hair.

101
00:06:43,650 --> 00:06:47,658
Influenced by China's own reform
movement, Lim urged Chinese

102
00:06:47,738 --> 00:06:50,587
men to cut off their
queues — the towchang,

103
00:06:50,667 --> 00:06:55,424
the plait imposed under the Qing —
as a symbol that the old order could

104
00:06:55,504 --> 00:06:59,512
be left behind, and he set out
his thinking in his 1901 book

105
00:06:59,592 --> 00:07:03,475
The Chinese Crisis from Within,
published as "Wen Ching".

106
00:07:03,475 --> 00:07:07,955
Around these causes sat
institution after institution:

107
00:07:08,035 --> 00:07:12,009
a Chinese newspaper in
1898, the Straits Chinese

108
00:07:12,089 --> 00:07:16,569
British Association in 1900,
and a founding membership

109
00:07:16,649 --> 00:07:21,125
of the Singapore Chinese
Chamber of Commerce in 1906.

110
00:07:21,125 --> 00:07:23,772
The reformer had a businessman's eye,

111
00:07:23,852 --> 00:07:26,800
and the money ran underneath the reform.

112
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:30,791
Henry Nicholas Ridley, the
director of the Botanic Gardens,

113
00:07:30,871 --> 00:07:35,150
was preaching rubber to anyone
who would listen; Lim listened.

114
00:07:35,150 --> 00:07:39,606
He invested early in Tan Chay
Yan's planting in Malacca,

115
00:07:39,686 --> 00:07:45,600
and in 1909 the two men formed Serangoon
Rubber Plantations in Singapore.

116
00:07:45,600 --> 00:07:48,221
Those who heeded his call to plant,

117
00:07:48,301 --> 00:07:51,850
BiblioAsia records,
enjoyed a booming success.

118
00:07:51,850 --> 00:07:53,775
Banking followed.

119
00:07:53,775 --> 00:07:58,413
Lim helped to establish all
three of the era's Hokkien banks:

120
00:07:58,493 --> 00:08:02,203
the Chinese Commercial
Bank in 1912, where he was

121
00:08:02,283 --> 00:08:06,534
vice-chairman at its founding;
the Ho Hong Bank in 1917,

122
00:08:06,614 --> 00:08:11,100
with Lim Peng Siang; and the
Oversea-Chinese Bank in 1919.

123
00:08:11,100 --> 00:08:15,272
Twenty years later, in the
depths of the Depression,

124
00:08:15,352 --> 00:08:19,605
the three amalgamated — on
the 31st of October 1932 —

125
00:08:19,685 --> 00:08:24,100
to form the Oversea-Chinese
Banking Corporation, OCBC.

126
00:08:24,100 --> 00:08:27,464
Lim himself had no hand in the merger:

127
00:08:27,544 --> 00:08:31,350
by 1932 he had been in China for a decade.

128
00:08:31,350 --> 00:08:35,200
What he co-founded were the
three banks that became it.

129
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:38,898
In 1920 he had also
co-founded, with Lim Nee Soon,

130
00:08:38,978 --> 00:08:44,112
the Overseas Assurance Corporation,
the first locally owned insurance

131
00:08:44,192 --> 00:08:48,575
company in Singapore, and
served as its founding chairman.

132
00:08:48,575 --> 00:08:52,050
China ran through Lim's middle years.

133
00:08:52,050 --> 00:08:55,764
He knew both Sun Yat-sen and
the reformer Kang Youwei,

134
00:08:55,844 --> 00:08:59,980
who visited Singapore in the
1900s; he was a founding member

135
00:09:00,060 --> 00:09:04,969
of the Singapore branch of Sun's
Tongmenghui, the Revolutionary League,

136
00:09:05,049 --> 00:09:07,639
set up in 1906, and when it became the

137
00:09:07,719 --> 00:09:12,075
Singapore branch of the Kuomintang
he served as its president.

138
00:09:12,075 --> 00:09:17,021
When the revolution overthrew the Qing
in 1911, the appointments followed:

139
00:09:17,101 --> 00:09:20,959
Lim was made personal physician
and confidential secretary

140
00:09:21,039 --> 00:09:25,034
to Sun Yat-sen, and was put in
charge of health services for

141
00:09:25,114 --> 00:09:29,109
the new republic — medical
adviser to the Peking government,

142
00:09:29,189 --> 00:09:33,875
inspector-general of its hospitals,
president of its Board of Health.

143
00:09:33,875 --> 00:09:36,852
In 1921 came the call that took him

144
00:09:36,932 --> 00:09:40,250
away from Singapore for sixteen years.

145
00:09:40,250 --> 00:09:44,624
Tan Kah Kee, the rubber and
pineapple magnate turned educator,

146
00:09:44,704 --> 00:09:49,366
had founded a university at Xiamen
— Amoy, in the older spelling —

147
00:09:49,446 --> 00:09:53,900
in his home province of Fujian,
and he invited Lim to lead it.

148
00:09:53,900 --> 00:09:58,157
Lim resigned from the
Legislative Council, gave up his

149
00:09:58,237 --> 00:10:01,932
Singapore business
interests, and served as the

150
00:10:02,012 --> 00:10:06,750
university's president from
1921 to 1937, without a salary.

151
00:10:06,750 --> 00:10:12,445
His best-known work of those years is
a scholar's, not an administrator's:

152
00:10:12,525 --> 00:10:15,879
his English translation
of Qu Yuan's Li Sao,

153
00:10:15,959 --> 00:10:21,500
published in 1929 with prefaces by
H. A. Giles and Rabindranath Tagore.

154
00:10:21,500 --> 00:10:25,265
In 1937, as the university
passed to government

155
00:10:25,345 --> 00:10:28,700
control and war approached, he came home.

156
00:10:28,700 --> 00:10:33,679
Home in 1937, Lim founded the
Straits Chinese China Relief Fund

157
00:10:33,759 --> 00:10:38,176
Committee to raise money for
China's war against Japan —

158
00:10:38,256 --> 00:10:43,475
which made him a marked man when
Singapore fell in February 1942.

159
00:10:43,475 --> 00:10:46,324
The Japanese administration pressured the

160
00:10:46,404 --> 00:10:50,682
seventy-two-year-old to head
the Oversea Chinese Association,

161
00:10:50,762 --> 00:10:54,398
the body charged with raising
the 50 million dollars

162
00:10:54,478 --> 00:10:58,050
the occupiers demanded
from the Chinese community.

163
00:10:58,050 --> 00:11:02,975
The record, as the National Library
Board tells it, is of an old man coerced:

164
00:11:03,055 --> 00:11:06,615
he at first refused; his wife
Grace was made to kneel in

165
00:11:06,695 --> 00:11:10,450
the sun for hours; he finally
accepted a figurehead's role,

166
00:11:10,530 --> 00:11:15,000
and then dragged his feet — the
heritage profile records that he often

167
00:11:15,080 --> 00:11:19,500
feigned drunkenness to avoid being
used further as a political tool.

168
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:24,250
When the money fell short, he
said so to the authorities' faces.

169
00:11:24,250 --> 00:11:27,127
"We never told a lie," he told them,

170
00:11:27,207 --> 00:11:31,150
as The Straits Times
recalled his words in 1947.

171
00:11:31,150 --> 00:11:34,725
"If we are unable to
pay, then die we will."

172
00:11:34,725 --> 00:11:37,858
Part of the sum was
raised; the balance was

173
00:11:37,938 --> 00:11:41,225
met by a loan from the
Yokohama Specie Bank.

174
00:11:41,225 --> 00:11:44,475
From 1937, Lim lived at 1 Paterson Hill,

175
00:11:44,555 --> 00:11:49,300
in the Orchard Road district,
in an attap-roofed mansion.

176
00:11:49,300 --> 00:11:52,561
Over the years the house
filled — with relatives,

177
00:11:52,641 --> 00:11:56,651
with dependants, with tenants
— until, in BiblioAsia's word,

178
00:11:56,731 --> 00:12:00,605
it had become his own "kampong",
a village of the extended

179
00:12:00,685 --> 00:12:04,423
household surviving into the
age of the apartment block,

180
00:12:04,503 --> 00:12:08,525
on some of the most valuable
residential land in Singapore.

181
00:12:08,525 --> 00:12:12,250
After the war he retired from public life.

182
00:12:12,250 --> 00:12:16,135
His last decade is, in the
record, essentially a blank:

183
00:12:16,215 --> 00:12:20,389
the Grand Old Man at home in
his kampong, while the city he

184
00:12:20,469 --> 00:12:24,650
had spent sixty years reforming
rebuilt itself around him.

185
00:12:24,650 --> 00:12:27,727
He died at home on the
1st of January 1957,

186
00:12:27,807 --> 00:12:34,188
leaving his wife Grace, six children, thirty
grandchildren and six great-grandchildren —

187
00:12:34,268 --> 00:12:36,977
among them, a generation further down,

188
00:12:37,057 --> 00:12:40,875
the playwright Stella Kon,
of Emily of Emerald Hill.

189
00:12:40,875 --> 00:12:44,833
He was buried at Bidadari
Cemetery; when the cemetery was

190
00:12:44,913 --> 00:12:49,368
cleared in the 2000s, his remains
were exhumed and his tombstone

191
00:12:49,448 --> 00:12:53,265
placed in the Bidadari Memorial
Garden at Mount Vernon,

192
00:12:53,345 --> 00:12:57,950
among a small company of the
notable dead the city chose to keep.

193
00:12:57,950 --> 00:13:03,025
Posterity has kept his name
in exactly two public places.

194
00:13:03,025 --> 00:13:06,000
The first is Boon Keng Road.

195
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:10,876
The second is the station beneath
it: Boon Keng MRT station,

196
00:13:10,956 --> 00:13:14,758
NE9 on the North East
Line, which opened on the

197
00:13:14,838 --> 00:13:19,050
20th of June 2003, forty-six
years after his death.

198
00:13:19,050 --> 00:13:22,988
In 2004, Senior Minister Lee
Kuan Yew held Lim up at an

199
00:13:23,068 --> 00:13:28,249
international conference as a role
model of biculturalism that Singapore

200
00:13:28,329 --> 00:13:33,217
Chinese should emulate — a man at
home in two civilisations at once,

201
00:13:33,297 --> 00:13:36,950
which is perhaps the
shortest summary of the life.

202
00:13:36,950 --> 00:13:40,375
The commuters get the
name without the life.

203
00:13:40,375 --> 00:13:43,986
It is the way of station
names; but this one belonged to

204
00:13:44,066 --> 00:13:47,678
a man who treated the poor
for free, fought the colony's

205
00:13:47,758 --> 00:13:51,172
most profitable vice, built
a school for girls nobody

206
00:13:51,252 --> 00:13:55,075
thought worth educating, and
ran a university for nothing.

207
00:13:55,075 --> 00:14:00,275
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Singapore names its stations after roads,

208
00:14:00,355 --> 00:14:03,550
and its roads after men
it has half forgotten.

209
00:14:03,550 --> 00:14:06,848
Lim Boon Keng's name
survives precisely where his

210
00:14:06,928 --> 00:14:10,157
life's work was aimed —
in the daily movement of

211
00:14:10,237 --> 00:14:12,845
ordinary people — attached to a doctor,

212
00:14:12,925 --> 00:14:17,475
reformer and educator whom almost
none of them could now describe.

213
00:14:17,475 --> 00:14:22,411
That gap, between the name everyone
uses and the life nobody remembers,

214
00:14:22,491 --> 00:14:25,175
is the most lesser-known thing of all.
